for me american citizenship is not just  being part of  um this culture this society not just  being part of  you know the place i've called home for  the last 20 years almost but also it's  it's about the values  that undergird the founding of this  country my name is alma or geniopari  and  i came  to america for the very first time at  the age of 19.  i grew up in ghana in west africa we  always wanted  to come to america as kids and so i've  been waiting almost in fact i've been  waiting half my life  to  become a citizen the process of becoming  a citizen is actually much much more  complicated and much longer  than most people realize and so for me i  came to the us for the first time  in  2003 i  came to los angeles as a missionary and  served for  two years as a missionary in los angeles  and i became a citizen  in 2021  and so the entire journey  took about 17 years to complete i  believe very strongly that america is  still the place where anybody  anybody  can make it  in spite of the challenges we have as a  country i personally went through  um quite a bit of struggle over the last  you know 18 years that i've been here by  many  many respects  you you could say that you know i have  achieved the american dream  and it didn't come overnight but i had  to make very specific and deliberate  decisions  one of those decisions was to get an  education  and then once i had the education  to make sure that i showed up  to make sure that i worked hard  to make sure that i i  made myself valuable to the people i was  providing my services to  and  then to take risks you know current  events  have  you know become such that a lot of  people  have either lost faith in in the  american experiment or are questioning  um the role of america in the world and  from my perspective as an immigrant i  love  america not because america is perfect  and i don't believe that but i believe  that america was founded on principles  that  are  necessary for the  flourishing of  man  and those principles are faith family  those principles are competition free  enterprise freedom of speech the ability  to protect and defend yourself it's  unfortunate that patriotism has become a  pejorative in some circles  and and now  you know it's being looked on as some  kind of  xenophobic idea  however as an immigrant  you know i admire american patriotism  patriotism was not something that we  thought about a lot in my home country  and most of the time  you know when we would speak about our  country we  we  had the habit of you know only pointing  out the problems that we face on a  regular basis  and coming to america and realizing that  you know loving your country  is not a pejorative loving your country  it's the the fuel  that pushes  the country forward  um a country with citizens that don't  love it  is destined i believe for failure the  love that people have for america  is not embedded in what the government  can give them i think the patriotism  that  many americans share  is rooted in the founding principles  of this country  if the source of your patriotism is  rooted  in those immutable principles of life  liberty and the pursuit of happiness  then  you can't be xenophobic because when you  see  other people seeking those same freedoms  you can't help  but welcome and usher them into that  circle  right i think what people resent  is  foreigners or other people or even  citizens  who  come here for the handouts  because that's not the essence of  patriotism  if i have any message for  young americans  my message to them which i have four  kids my message to them is that  you know you  hold the key  to the limits of your success  [Music]  you
 

